 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Bridge outputs and gain",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Each channel is two amplifiers driving the speaker from both ends, one non-inverting and one inverting, both referenced to an internal Vref. Gain is fixed inside at 32 dB; no supply-ripple capacitor, bootstrap capacitors or output RC networks are needed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Stand-by and mute sequencing",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Turn-on goes off, stand-by, mute, play; turn-off reverses it. Pin 7 is raised through an RC so the bias circuits start slowly; when it passes the stand-by threshold (1.3 V typ) the amplifier switches on and the input coupling capacitors charge. Pin 6 is held below the mute threshold (2.9 V typ) until that charging ends; entering play earlier gives a loud pop at the speakers. 100 ms to 200 ms between the stand-by and mute signals is enough."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Control signal timing",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "With an 18 V supply, the pin 7 voltage crosses the 0.8 V / 1.3 V / 1.8 V stand-by threshold band and quiescent current appears; pin 6 then crosses the 2.3 V / 2.9 V / 4.1 V mute threshold band and the output starts. On the way down, mute is pulled low before stand-by and before the supply is removed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply-switched operation",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Without a controller the two control pins are joined and fed from the supply through a divider; the capacitor on the joined node (C4) holds both pins below threshold for a while after power is applied."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Protection",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Outputs are short-circuit protected and the output current is limited internally to 2 A peak. A thermal shutdown acts at about 150 °C junction."
  }
 ],
